Salary and pay period are pulled out of the posting text with a set of
pattern matchers, refined by hand against a large corpus of real postings. A
match always requires actual digits, so a line like
"Compensation: Competitive + Incentives" never gets read as a number. When
a posting names a figure but not a period, the magnitude alone is usually
enough to tell apart a yearly salary from an hourly rate:

```go
func inferPeriod(s *Salary) Period {
	top := 0.0
	if s.Maximum != nil {
		top = *s.Maximum
	} else if s.Minimum != nil {
		top = *s.Minimum
	}
	switch {
	case top >= 20_000:
		return PeriodAnnual
	case top >= 7 && top <= 400:
		return PeriodHourly
	case s.Currency == EUR && top >= 1000 && top < 10_000:
		return PeriodMonthly
	}
	return ""
}
```

Each source syncs on its own schedule, with boards spaced evenly across the
run so a slow one never holds up the others — a board that takes longer
than its slot just delays the next one instead of stalling the whole sync:

```go
var slot time.Duration
if len(boards) > 0 {
	slot = spread / time.Duration(len(boards))
}
started := time.Now()
for i, b := range boards {
	if !sleep(ctx, time.Until(started.Add(time.Duration(i)*slot))) {
		return
	}
	syncBoard(ctx, st, b, fetch)
}
```

With everything in one place, it's also a live read on the market itself —
a world map of where postings show up, switchable between raw density and
median salary by location.

